"""Quantum channel, circuit, simulator, sensor, algorithm, bus, cellular automaton, complexity, computing, cryptography, engineering."""
NARRATIVES = {
    "quantum_channel": [
        ("p", r"An excited atom in free space emits a photon that flies away undetected. The state of the atom afterwards follows from the joint unitary evolution of atom and radiation field, after which the field is ignored. Let $|g\rangle$ and $|e\rangle$ be the ground and excited states, let the field start in its vacuum $|0\rangle_E$, and let $\Gamma$ be the spontaneous emission rate. During a time $t$ the excitation leaves the atom with probability $\gamma=1-e^{-\Gamma t}$:"),
        ("eq", r"""U|g\rangle|0\rangle_E=|g\rangle|0\rangle_E,\qquad
U|e\rangle|0\rangle_E=\sqrt{1-\gamma}\,|e\rangle|0\rangle_E+\sqrt{\gamma}\,|g\rangle|1\rangle_E."""),
        ("p", r"Tracing out the field gives the density operator of the atom alone. Each orthogonal field state $|k\rangle_E$ contributes one term, with the Kraus operator $K_k={}_E\langle k|U|0\rangle_E$. Unitarity of $U$ makes the Kraus operators complete, which is conservation of probability. Any later measurement with operators $M_y$ then has outcome probabilities $p(y)=\operatorname{Tr}[M_y\mathcal E(\rho)]$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathcal E(\rho)=\sum_k K_k\rho K_k^\dagger,\qquad \sum_k K_k^\dagger K_k=I,\\
K_0=|g\rangle\langle g|+\sqrt{1-\gamma}\,|e\rangle\langle e|,\qquad K_1=\sqrt{\gamma}\,|g\rangle\langle e|."""),
        ("p", r"In the basis $(|g\rangle,|e\rangle)$ this amplitude-damping channel moves population downward and shrinks the coherence by a smaller factor:"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathcal E\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{gg}&\rho_{ge}\\ \rho_{eg}&\rho_{ee}\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{gg}+\gamma\rho_{ee}&\sqrt{1-\gamma}\,\rho_{ge}\\
\sqrt{1-\gamma}\,\rho_{eg}&(1-\gamma)\rho_{ee}\end{pmatrix}."""),
        ("p", r"The excited population decays as $e^{-\Gamma t}$, while the coherence decays as $\sqrt{1-\gamma}=e^{-\Gamma t/2}$. Energy relaxation alone therefore gives a coherence time $T_2=2T_1$, with $T_1=1/\Gamma$. Measured atomic and superconducting qubits obey $T_2\leq2T_1$, and any shortfall comes from additional dephasing, such as slow fluctuations of the transition frequency. Two successive damping intervals compose as $1-\gamma=(1-\gamma_1)(1-\gamma_2)$, consistent with the exponential law."),
        ("p", r"The Kraus operators are not unique. A random phase flip $Z$ applied with probability $p$ and an unrecorded measurement in the $Z$ basis made with probability $2p$ produce the same channel. Both multiply the coherence by $1-2p$. Two Kraus sets of one channel are always related by a unitary mixing of their operators. Physically, this corresponds to measuring the discarded environment in a different basis."),
        ("p", r"A channel must remain physical when its input is entangled with an untouched partner, and positivity of $\mathcal E(\rho)$ for single inputs does not ensure this. Matrix transposition $T$ maps every density operator to a density operator. Applied to one half of the Bell state $|\Phi^+\rangle=(|00\rangle+|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$, however, it gives"),
        ("eq", r"""(\mathrm{id}\otimes T)\big(|\Phi^+\rangle\langle\Phi^+|\big)=\tfrac12\,\mathrm{SWAP},
\qquad \text{eigenvalues } \tfrac12,\tfrac12,\tfrac12,-\tfrac12."""),
        ("p", r"The negative eigenvalue would be a negative probability, so no physical process acts as a transposition. The test generalizes: a linear map is completely positive exactly when its Choi matrix $\sum_{ij}|i\rangle\langle j|\otimes\mathcal E(|i\rangle\langle j|)$ is positive. For amplitude damping the eigenvalues are $0$, $0$, $\gamma$ and $2-\gamma$."),
        ("p", r"The description assumes that the atom and field start uncorrelated and that emitted light never returns. A mirror that reflects the photon back to the atom violates both conditions and makes the evolution depend on its history. Over a short interval, $\gamma=\Gamma\,dt$ turns $K_1$ into the jump operator of the quantum jump description. Quantum error correction asks which channels of this kind can be undone on an encoded subspace."),
    ],
    "quantum_circuit": [
        ("p", r"Two laboratory operations on a pair of qubits, a Hadamard pulse on one and a controlled-NOT between them, give different results in the two possible orders. In one order the qubits end entangled, with perfectly correlated outcomes; in the other they remain uncorrelated. A quantum circuit specifies such orders. Each gate is a unitary $U_j$ acting on a few qubits, and the register evolves by the time-ordered product:"),
        ("eq", r"""\rho_{\rm out}=U_m\cdots U_2U_1\,\rho_{\rm in}\,U_1^\dagger U_2^\dagger\cdots U_m^\dagger,
\qquad p(y)=\operatorname{Tr}(M_y\rho_{\rm out})."""),
        ("p", r"Here $M_y$ is the measurement operator for outcome $y$, usually a projector on a computational basis state. The Hadamard gate is $H=(X+Z)/\sqrt2$, and the controlled-NOT flips the target qubit when the control qubit is in $|1\rangle$. Starting from $|00\rangle$, the two orders give"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathrm{CNOT}\,(H\otimes I)|00\rangle=\frac{|00\rangle+|11\rangle}{\sqrt2},\qquad
(H\otimes I)\,\mathrm{CNOT}|00\rangle=\frac{|00\rangle+|10\rangle}{\sqrt2}."""),
        ("p", r"In the left state, $Z$ measurements on the two qubits always agree. In the right state, the target qubit is always found in $0$ and the control qubit is random. The circuit diagram is therefore a record of the time order of physical couplings, not a list of available operations."),
        ("p", r"The roles of control and target depend on the basis in which a gate is viewed. Let $|-\rangle=(|0\rangle-|1\rangle)/\sqrt2$, an eigenstate of $X$ with eigenvalue $-1$. Flipping the target then leaves it unchanged except for a sign, which appears as a phase of the control:"),
        ("eq", r"""\mathrm{CNOT}\,|x\rangle|-\rangle=(-1)^x|x\rangle|-\rangle,\qquad
(H\otimes H)\,\mathrm{CNOT}_{12}\,(H\otimes H)=\mathrm{CNOT}_{21}."""),
        ("p", r"The reversal of control and target follows from the kickback, because Hadamards exchange the $X$ and $Z$ bases on both qubits. This phase kickback is the step by which an oracle writes a function value into a phase, as in the Deutsch--Jozsa circuit described under quantum computing."),
        ("p", r"Single-qubit rotations together with the controlled-NOT suffice to compose any unitary on $n$ qubits (Barenco and colleagues, 1995). Universality says nothing about cost. A general unitary on $n$ qubits has $4^n-1$ real parameters, while each gate supplies a bounded number. Most unitaries therefore require a number of gates that grows exponentially with $n$. Efficient algorithms are the special unitaries with circuits of polynomial size."),
        ("p", r"The circuit description assumes that each gate acts only on its intended qubits and that the qubits do not leak into other levels. Noise is then represented by a quantum channel after each gate. If errors are independent with probability $\epsilon$ per gate, a circuit of $n_g$ gates runs without any error with probability"),
        ("eq", r"""P_{\rm ok}=(1-\epsilon)^{n_g}\approx e^{-n_g\epsilon}."""),
        ("p", r"With $\epsilon=0.005$ per two-qubit gate, a circuit of 200 such gates runs without error with probability $0.37$. This fixes the depth that can be reached without quantum error correction. Error correction replaces the exponential decay by repeated syndrome measurements and corrections, which are themselves further gates of the circuit."),
    ],
    "quantum_simulator": [
        ("p", r"A chain of fifty interacting spins has $2^{50}\approx10^{15}$ amplitudes, about 18 petabytes in double precision. A controllable quantum device can instead be made to follow the same dynamics, for example trapped ions with laser-driven spin couplings or ultracold atoms in optical lattices. In an analog simulator the device Hamiltonian is engineered to match the target. In a digital simulator the target evolution is decomposed into gates."),
        ("p", r"Let the target Hamiltonian be a sum $H=A+B$ of two parts that the device can apply separately, such as spin couplings $A$ and a transverse field $B$. Set $\hbar=1$, divide the time $t$ into $n$ steps of length $\delta=t/n$, and alternate the two evolutions. The Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff expansion of one step gives"),
        ("eq", r"""e^{-iA\delta}e^{-iB\delta}=\exp\!\Big[-i(A+B)\delta-\frac{\delta^2}{2}[A,B]+O(\delta^3)\Big]."""),
        ("p", r"Each step therefore carries an error of size $\delta^2\|[A,B]\|/2$, where $\|\cdot\|$ is the operator norm. Unitary evolution preserves norms, so the errors of the $n$ steps add at most linearly:"),
        ("eq", r"""\Big\|e^{-i(A+B)t}-\big(e^{-iAt/n}e^{-iBt/n}\big)^n\Big\|\leq\frac{t^2}{2n}\,\big\|[A,B]\big\|."""),
        ("p", r"The error falls as $1/n$ and vanishes when $A$ and $B$ commute. A symmetric step places a half step of $A$ on each side of a full step of $B$. It cancels the commutator term and leaves an error of order $t^3/n^2$, set by double commutators."),
        ("p", r"For two spins with $A=JZ_1Z_2$ and $B=h(X_1+X_2)$, the commutator is $[A,B]=2iJh(Y_1Z_2+Z_1Y_2)$, with norm $4Jh$. For $J=h$, $Jt=1$ and ten steps the bound is $0.2$. Direct computation gives an actual error of $0.071$, which halves when the number of steps doubles. The symmetric step gives $0.006$. For a ring of $N$ spins the triangle inequality bounds the commutator by $4NJh$, so the required number of steps grows in proportion to $NJht^2/\epsilon$ for a target error $\epsilon$."),
        ("p", r"Real gates are imperfect. If every step adds an error of at most $\epsilon_s$, the total error is bounded by $at^2/n+n\epsilon_s$ with $a=\|[A,B]\|/2$. The bound is smallest at $n^*=t\sqrt{a/\epsilon_s}$, where it equals $2t\sqrt{a\epsilon_s}$. Beyond this number of steps, finer time steps make the simulation worse."),
        ("p", r"A simulation claim also requires an encoding $V$ of target states into device states, with $V^\dagger V=I$. If the device evolution differs from the encoded target evolution by at most $\varepsilon(t)$, every bounded observable $O$ inherits a bounded error:"),
        ("eq", r"""\big\|U_{\rm dev}(t)V-VU_{\rm tar}(t)\big\|\leq\varepsilon(t)\ \Longrightarrow\
\big|\langle O\rangle_{\rm tar}-\langle VOV^\dagger\rangle_{\rm dev}\big|\leq2\|O\|\,\varepsilon(t)."""),
        ("p", r"Analog simulators are validated differently. In the experiment of Greiner and colleagues (2002), atoms in an optical lattice realized the Bose--Hubbard model. Raising the lattice depth drove the transition from a superfluid to a Mott insulator, seen as the loss of interference peaks after release from the lattice. No Trotter bound applies there. The error budget comes instead from calibrating tunnelling, interaction and residual trapping against independent measurements. The commutator that sets the Trotter error is the same one that makes gate order matter in a quantum circuit."),
    ],
    "quantum_sensor": [
        ("p", r"A nitrogen-vacancy centre in diamond, a trapped ion or an atom in a vapour cell detects a magnetic field through the precession of its spin. A quantum sensor is a probe with well-separated levels whose energy difference depends on the quantity to be measured. The signal accumulates as a relative phase between two states, which is then converted into a population that a detector counts."),
        ("p", r"In a Ramsey sequence the probe starts in $|0\rangle$, and a $\pi/2$ pulse creates an equal superposition of $|0\rangle$ and $|1\rangle$. During a free evolution time $\tau$, a detuning $\delta$ between the transition and a reference oscillator adds the relative phase $\delta\tau$. A second $\pi/2$ pulse converts this phase into the probability of finding the excited state. For $\nu$ independent probes or repetitions, binomial counting adds projection noise:"),
        ("eq", r"""P_1=\frac{1+\cos\delta\tau}{2},\qquad \Delta P_1=\sqrt{\frac{P_1(1-P_1)}{\nu}},\qquad
\Delta\delta=\frac{\Delta P_1}{|\partial P_1/\partial\delta|}=\frac{1}{\tau\sqrt\nu}."""),
        ("p", r"The frequency uncertainty is independent of the operating point and defines the standard quantum limit for uncorrelated probes. A longer phase accumulation improves it until the coherence time $T_2$ limits $\tau$. Correlated probe states can exceed this limit, as described under quantum metrology."),
        ("p", r"A different sensor records a weak field in the choice between two macroscopic states. A Kerr parametric oscillator, such as a Josephson microwave resonator pumped at twice its frequency, has two stable oscillation states of opposite phase above a threshold. Swept through the threshold, it amplifies vacuum fluctuations into one of the two states with equal probability. A resonant bias $h$ far below one photon tilts that choice. In the frame rotating at half the pump frequency, with $\hbar=1$,"),
        ("eq", r"""H=-Ka^{\dagger2}a^2+\frac{i\epsilon_2(t)}{2}\big(a^{\dagger2}-a^2\big)+ih\big(a^\dagger-a\big),\\
\dot\rho=-i[H,\rho]+\kappa(\bar n+1)\mathcal D[a]\rho+\kappa\bar n\,\mathcal D[a^\dagger]\rho."""),
        ("p", r"Here $K$ is the Kerr coefficient, $\epsilon_2=rt$ the two-photon drive swept at rate $r$, $\kappa$ the loss rate, $\bar n$ the thermal occupation of the bath, and $\mathcal D[a]\rho=a\rho a^\dagger-\{a^\dagger a,\rho\}/2$. Above threshold, $\epsilon_2>\kappa/2$, each stored state holds $\sqrt{\epsilon_2^2-\kappa^2/4}/(2K)$ photons. While the amplitude is small the Kerr term is negligible. A Gaussian Wigner function evolving under a quadratic Hamiltonian with linear loss obeys exactly the classical Fokker--Planck equation. The amplified quadrature $x=\mathrm{Re}\,\alpha$ therefore follows a classical noisy equation:"),
        ("eq", r"""\dot x=\big(\epsilon_2-\tfrac{\kappa}{2}\big)x+h+\sqrt{2D}\,\xi(t),\qquad 2D=\frac{\kappa(2\bar n+1)}{4},\\
P=\Phi\!\left(\frac{hI_1}{\sqrt{\sigma_0^2+2DI_2}}\right),\qquad I_k=\int_0^\infty e^{-k\phi(s)}ds,\qquad
\phi(s)=\frac{rs^2-\kappa s}{2}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $\xi$ is unit white noise, $\sigma_0^2=(2\bar n_0+1)/4$ the initial variance of $x$, and $\Phi$ the normal distribution function. The bias shifts the mean of the amplified seed, the noise sets its width, and $P$ is the probability of the favoured state. The noise strength $D$ is not a free parameter; vacuum and thermal fluctuations fix it. Evaluating the integrals gives two limits:"),
        ("eq", r"""\kappa\gg\sqrt r:\ P=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\pi^{1/4}h}{D^{1/2}r^{1/4}}\right),\qquad
\kappa\to0:\ P=\Phi\!\left(h\sqrt{2\pi/r}\right)."""),
        ("p", r"The strong-loss limit is the selection law of a classical swept pitchfork, derived by Kondepudi and Nelson in 1985, here with the noise fixed by the vacuum. Loss renews the seed during the sweep, and the rate enters with the exponent $-1/4$. Without loss the seed is the initial vacuum, and the exponent becomes $-1/2$. A higher temperature lowers the argument of $\Phi$ as $(2\bar n+1)^{-1/2}$. Gu and colleagues derived the corresponding relation for a sudden step of the gain. Roques-Carmes and colleagues measured it in an optical parametric oscillator, where bias pulses below one photon set the two probabilities."),
        ("p", r"In units where $\kappa=1$, with $r=0.01$ and $h=0.108$, the law predicts $P=0.90$. Exact density-operator evolution at 27 stored photons gives $0.900$, within $0.001$ of the law. The law requires the choice to be complete before the Kerr term acts, which fails at few photons. With $K=\kappa$ and 1.5 stored photons the same sweep gives $0.68$, because the oscillator switches between its shallow wells while the sweep proceeds. The probability then relaxes toward the selection $P_{\rm eq}$ of the biased steady state, at the slowest decay rate $\Gamma$ of the Lindblad equation:"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac{dp}{dt}=\Gamma(\epsilon_2)\big[P_{\rm eq}(\epsilon_2)-p\big]."""),
        ("p", r"This one-mode relaxation reproduces the exact few-photon probability within $0.01$ over two decades of sweep rate. The rate dependence separates the regimes: a probability that rises for slower sweeps comes from the Gaussian stage, and a rate-independent probability from equilibrating wells. Yamaji and colleagues (2025) measured the probability of the favoured state against a one-photon bias in a Josephson oscillator holding about twelve photons. A related selection under a residual bias, studied against the crossing rate, occurs at the Dicke transition of a Bose--Einstein condensate in an optical cavity."),
    ],
    "quantum_algorithm": [
        ("p", r"A list of $N$ entries contains one marked entry, and the only access is a test that answers whether a given entry is marked. A classical search needs about $N/2$ tests on average. Grover's algorithm finds the entry with about $(\pi/4)\sqrt N$ tests by rotating a quantum state, step by step, toward the answer."),
        ("p", r"Hadamard gates prepare a register of $n=\log_2N$ qubits in the uniform superposition $|s\rangle=N^{-1/2}\sum_x|x\rangle$. Let $|w\rangle$ be the marked state and $|r\rangle$ the normalized superposition of the other $N-1$ states. Then $|s\rangle=\sin\theta\,|w\rangle+\cos\theta\,|r\rangle$ with $\sin\theta=1/\sqrt N$. The test acts as a phase oracle $O$ that flips the sign of the marked amplitude, and a second operation $D$ reflects about $|s\rangle$:"),
        ("eq", r"""O=I-2|w\rangle\langle w|,\qquad D=2|s\rangle\langle s|-I,\qquad G=DO."""),
        ("p", r"Both reflections map the plane spanned by $|w\rangle$ and $|r\rangle$ into itself. The oracle reflects about $|r\rangle$ and $D$ reflects about $|s\rangle$. Two reflections about lines separated by the angle $\theta$ combine into a rotation by $2\theta$. After $k$ iterations the state and the probability of finding the marked entry are"),
        ("eq", r"""G^k|s\rangle=\sin\big((2k+1)\theta\big)|w\rangle+\cos\big((2k+1)\theta\big)|r\rangle,\qquad
P_k=\sin^2\big((2k+1)\theta\big)."""),
        ("p", r"The probability is nearest one when $(2k+1)\theta\approx\pi/2$, after $k\approx\pi/(4\theta)-1/2\approx(\pi/4)\sqrt N$ iterations. The oracle sign flip is realized with an ancilla in the state $|-\rangle$, by the phase kickback described under quantum circuit. The reflection $D$ equals $H^{\otimes n}(2|0\rangle\langle0|-I)H^{\otimes n}$, a sign flip of the all-zero state between two layers of Hadamards."),
        ("p", r"For $N=4$ the angle is $\theta=30^\circ$. One iteration gives $(2k+1)\theta=90^\circ$, so the marked entry is found with certainty after a single query. A classical strategy with one query followed by a guess succeeds with probability one half. Chuang, Gershenfeld and Kubinec ran this two-qubit search with nuclear spins in 1998. For $N=2^{20}$, 804 iterations give a success probability above $0.9999997$, against about half a million classical tests on average."),
        ("p", r"The rotation does not stop at the answer. Continuing beyond the optimum turns the state past $|w\rangle$; for $N=16$, six iterations give a success probability of only $0.02$, while three give $0.96$. The number of marked entries must therefore be known, or estimated from the eigenphases $\pm2\theta$ of $G$ by phase estimation."),
        ("p", r"The same geometry applies to any preparation circuit $\mathcal A$ that succeeds with probability $a=\sin^2\theta$. Alternating a reflection that flags success with a reflection about $\mathcal A|0\rangle$ raises the success probability close to one:"),
        ("eq", r"""k\approx\frac{\pi}{4\sqrt a}\ \text{rounds}\qquad\text{instead of}\qquad\sim\frac1a\ \text{classical repetitions}."""),
        ("p", r"This amplitude amplification is a quadratic gain, and for unstructured search it cannot be improved, as shown under quantum complexity theory. In practice noise limits it. Every iteration needs an oracle circuit and an $n$-qubit reflection, and errors accumulated over about $\sqrt N$ iterations dephase the coherent rotation."),
    ],
    "quantum_bus": [
        ("p", r"Two superconducting qubits on a chip, several millimetres apart, have no direct coupling. Both can couple to one microwave resonator that runs between them. When the qubits are detuned from the resonator, it is excited only virtually, yet it transmits an exchange interaction between them. The resonator then acts as a quantum bus, as demonstrated by Majer and colleagues in 2007."),
        ("p", r"Let qubits $j=1,2$ have transition frequencies $\omega_j$ and couplings $g_j$ to a resonator mode $a$ of frequency $\omega_r$, with detunings $\Delta_j=\omega_j-\omega_r$. With $\hbar=1$ and the rotating-wave approximation, the Tavis--Cummings Hamiltonian is"),
        ("eq", r"""H=\omega_ra^\dagger a+\sum_{j=1,2}\Big[\frac{\omega_j}{2}\sigma_z^{(j)}
+g_j\big(a\sigma_+^{(j)}+a^\dagger\sigma_-^{(j)}\big)\Big]."""),
        ("p", r"Consider the dispersive regime $|\Delta_j|\gg g_j$. The state $|e,g,0\rangle$, with the excitation on qubit 1, couples to $|g,e,0\rangle$ only through the intermediate state $|g,g,1\rangle$. In that state the photon sits in the resonator. The amplitude leaves qubit 1 with strength $g_1$, pays the energy denominator $\Delta$, and arrives at qubit 2 with strength $g_2$. Second-order perturbation theory, or a Schrieffer--Wolff transformation, eliminates the resonator. With the resonator in its vacuum,"),
        ("eq", r"""H_{\rm eff}=\sum_j\frac{\omega_j+\chi_j}{2}\sigma_z^{(j)}
+J\big(\sigma_+^{(1)}\sigma_-^{(2)}+\sigma_-^{(1)}\sigma_+^{(2)}\big),\\
\chi_j=\frac{g_j^2}{\Delta_j},\qquad J=\frac{g_1g_2}{2}\Big(\frac{1}{\Delta_1}+\frac{1}{\Delta_2}\Big)."""),
        ("p", r"For equal detunings $\Delta_1=\Delta_2=\Delta$ the exchange rate is $J=g_1g_2/\Delta$. The shifts $\chi_j$ also move each qubit frequency. When the shifted frequencies coincide, an excitation prepared on qubit 1 oscillates to qubit 2:"),
        ("eq", r"""P_{1\to2}(t)=\sin^2(Jt),\qquad t_{\rm swap}=\frac{\pi}{2J}."""),
        ("p", r"With $g/2\pi=100$ MHz and $\Delta/2\pi=1$ GHz, the exchange is $J/2\pi=10$ MHz, and a full transfer takes $25$ ns. Exact diagonalization of the three single-excitation states gives a splitting within 2 percent of $2J$ for these values. At $\Delta=5g$ the deviation grows to 7 percent, a correction of relative order $(g/\Delta)^2$. Stopping the transfer halfway, at $t=\pi/(4J)$, produces an entangling gate of the $\sqrt{i\mathrm{SWAP}}$ type. Detuning the qubits from each other by much more than $J$ switches the exchange off, because it no longer conserves energy."),
        ("p", r"The derivation needs $g_j\ll|\Delta_j|$ and a resonator photon number below the critical value $n_{\rm crit}=\Delta^2/(4g^2)$. Terms dropped in the rotating-wave approximation contribute with denominators $\omega_j+\omega_r$ and are small when $|\Delta_j|\ll\omega_j+\omega_r$. The bus also costs coherence. Resonator loss at rate $\kappa$ makes each qubit decay at the Purcell rate $\kappa g^2/\Delta^2$."),
        ("p", r"Beyond the vacuum, the qubit frequency shift becomes $\chi(2a^\dagger a+1)$, so the resonator frequency depends on the qubit state. A microwave tone transmitted through the resonator then measures the qubit without exchanging energy with it. The bus is the Jaynes--Cummings coupling of quantum optics applied to two qubits at once, and it supplies two-qubit gates for a quantum circuit."),
    ],
    "quantum_cellular_automaton": [
        ("p", r"A particle hops on a lattice in discrete time steps, and its internal two-level state is rotated before each hop. It spreads in a way no classical random walk can. The rule is local, identical at every site and unitary. Such rules define quantum cellular automata: lattices of finite-dimensional cells updated by one global unitary $U$ that is translation invariant and causal. Causality means that for any operator $A_x$ on cell $x$, the evolved operator $U^\dagger A_xU$ acts only on a fixed neighbourhood of $x$."),
        ("p", r"The simplest automaton carries one particle with a two-level internal state, the coin. Each step applies a coin rotation $C(\theta)$ and then moves the particle one site right if the coin points up and one site left if it points down. Translation invariance lets plane waves with quasimomentum $k$ evolve independently:"),
        ("eq", r"""U(k)=\begin{pmatrix}e^{-ik}&0\\0&e^{ik}\end{pmatrix}C(\theta),\qquad
C(\theta)=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\ \sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{pmatrix}."""),
        ("p", r"Since $\det U(k)=1$, its eigenvalues are $e^{\pm i\omega(k)}$ with $2\cos\omega=\operatorname{Tr}U(k)$. The dispersion relation and the group velocity follow directly:"),
        ("eq", r"""\cos\omega(k)=\cos\theta\cos k,\qquad v(k)=\frac{d\omega}{dk}=\frac{\cos\theta\sin k}{\sin\omega(k)}."""),
        ("p", r"The group velocity reaches its largest value $\cos\theta$ at $k=\pi/2$. The shift itself imposes a strict light cone of one site per step. A particle started at one site has a flat distribution of $k$, and both bands share the same $v^2$. Its spread therefore grows ballistically, with a coefficient set by the average of $v^2$ over the Brillouin zone:"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac{\langle x^2\rangle}{t^2}\ \xrightarrow{\ t\to\infty\ }\ \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}v(k)^2\,dk=1-|\sin\theta|,
\qquad\text{classical walk: }\langle x^2\rangle=t."""),
        ("p", r"For the balanced coin $\theta=\pi/4$, whose spreading equals that of the Hadamard walk, the standard deviation is $\sigma\approx0.54\,t$. After 100 steps the distribution is about 54 sites wide, against 10 sites for an unbiased classical random walk. A direct simulation of 400 steps gives $\sigma/t=0.5412$, and no probability appears outside the light cone. Karski and colleagues (2009) implemented such a walk with single neutral atoms in a spin-dependent optical lattice and imaged the final distribution site by site."),
        ("p", r"Near $k=0$ and for small $\theta$, the dispersion becomes $\omega^2\approx k^2+\theta^2$. With lattice spacing $a$ and time step $\tau$ this is the relativistic relation $E^2=p^2c^2+m^2c^4$, with $c=a/\tau$ and $mc^2=\hbar\theta/\tau$. The automaton is a discretization of the one-dimensional Dirac equation that keeps exact unitarity and a strict light cone."),
        ("p", r"For many interacting cells the same definition applies. Schumacher and Werner showed that every such automaton is obtained from two layers of blockwise unitaries in a generalized Margolus partitioning of the lattice. In one dimension, an index measures the net flow of quantum information through the lattice (Gross, Nesme, Vogts and Werner). A shift of qubits by one cell has index 2, while every finite-depth circuit of local gates on fixed cells has index 1. A translation is therefore local and reversible, yet no such circuit produces it. The classification in three and more dimensions remains open."),
        ("p", r"The strict light cone is the discrete counterpart of the Lieb--Robinson bound for lattice Hamiltonians. A digital quantum simulator built from Trotter steps is a circuit of the same layered form, and its gates act within the same kind of light cone."),
    ],
    "quantum_complexity_theory": [
        ("p", r"Grover's algorithm finds one marked entry among $N$ with about $(\pi/4)\sqrt N$ queries. Whether a cleverer quantum algorithm could use far fewer is a statement about all possible circuits, not about one of them. Quantum complexity theory answers such questions by fixing a model of computation and counting resources."),
        ("p", r"The standard model is a uniform family of circuits. For each input length $m$, a classical procedure running in time polynomial in $m$ writes down a circuit of polynomially many gates from a fixed universal set. The circuit acts on the input and on ancillas prepared in $|0\rangle$, and one qubit is measured at the end. A decision problem belongs to BQP, bounded-error quantum polynomial time, when the correct answer appears with probability at least $2/3$ for every input:"),
        ("eq", r"""\Pr[\text{output}=\text{answer}]\geq\frac23,\qquad
\Pr[\text{majority of }k\text{ runs wrong}]\leq e^{-k/18}."""),
        ("p", r"The constant $2/3$ is conventional. Repeating the circuit $k$ times and taking the majority lowers the error exponentially by the Hoeffding inequality, so 373 repetitions bring it below $10^{-9}$. Classical bounded-error probabilistic computation, BPP, is contained in BQP. BQP is contained in PSPACE, because each outcome probability can be computed as a sum over paths using polynomial memory. Factoring lies in BQP through Shor's algorithm and has no known efficient classical algorithm. Whether BQP is strictly larger than BPP is not proven."),
        ("p", r"Lower bounds can be proven in the query model, where the input is a black box. For search, the oracle $O_x$ flips the sign of the query basis state $|x\rangle$. Any algorithm alternates fixed unitaries with $T$ oracle calls. Compare a run with the oracle for marked entry $x$ against a run in which every oracle call is replaced by the identity. Let $|\psi_t\rangle$ be the state of the second run after $t$ calls, and $\alpha_{x,t}$ the norm of its component with query input $x$, so that $\sum_x|\alpha_{x,t}|^2=1$. One call can separate the two runs by at most $2|\alpha_{x,t}|$, and the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality then bounds the total separation:"),
        ("eq", r"""\big\|\psi_T^x-\psi_T\big\|\leq2\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}|\alpha_{x,t}|,\qquad
\sum_x\big\|\psi_T^x-\psi_T\big\|^2\leq4T\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\sum_x|\alpha_{x,t}|^2=4T^2."""),
        ("p", r"Success requires that the final state for each $x$ outputs $x$ with probability at least $2/3$. For at least half of the values $x$, the run without a marked entry outputs $x$ with probability at most $2/N$. For each such $x$ the two final states differ in norm by at least $2/3-2/N$, which gives"),
        ("eq", r"""\frac N2\Big(\frac23-\frac2N\Big)^2\leq4T^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
T\geq\sqrt{\frac N8}\Big(\frac23-\frac2N\Big)\approx0.24\sqrt N."""),
        ("p", r"For $N=10^6$ entries any quantum algorithm needs at least 236 queries. Grover's algorithm uses 785, and a classical search about $5\times10^5$ on average. The square-root gain is therefore optimal up to a constant factor, and Zalka showed that $(\pi/4)\sqrt N$ is exactly optimal for near-certain success. Grover's algorithm almost saturates the inequality: for $N=1024$ and 25 iterations the sum of squared separations is 2032, against the bound 2500."),
        ("p", r"This hybrid argument is due to Bennett, Bernstein, Brassard and Vazirani (1997). It shows that unstructured search admits no exponential quantum speedup. It also yields an oracle relative to which NP is not contained in BQP, although the unrelativized question remains open. Problems with algebraic structure escape the argument; period finding in Shor's algorithm is the standard example. The matching upper bound is the amplitude-amplification angle under quantum algorithm, and the polynomial-size condition counts gates of a quantum circuit."),
    ],
    "quantum_computing": [
        ("p", r"A function $f$ on $n$-bit strings returns $0$ or $1$ and is promised to be either constant or balanced, giving $0$ on exactly half of its inputs. A deterministic classical procedure may need $2^{n-1}+1$ evaluations to decide which. A quantum computer decides it with one evaluation, through interference among all $2^n$ inputs. This Deutsch--Jozsa calculation shows where the advantage of quantum computing comes from, and where it does not."),
        ("p", r"The register holds $n$ qubits and one ancilla. The function is accessed through the unitary $U_f|x\rangle|y\rangle=|x\rangle|y\oplus f(x)\rangle$, where $\oplus$ is addition modulo two. With the ancilla in $|-\rangle$, phase kickback turns the call into the sign $(-1)^{f(x)}$. Hadamard gates on all $n$ qubits before and after the call give"),
        ("eq", r"""|0\rangle^{\otimes n}\ \xrightarrow{H^{\otimes n}}\ \frac{1}{2^{n/2}}\sum_x|x\rangle
\ \xrightarrow{U_f}\ \frac{1}{2^{n/2}}\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}|x\rangle
\ \xrightarrow{H^{\otimes n}}\ \sum_yc_y|y\rangle,"""),
        ("eq", r"""c_y=\frac{1}{2^n}\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)+x\cdot y},\qquad c_{0}=\frac{1}{2^n}\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $x\cdot y$ is the bitwise inner product modulo two. For a constant function $c_0=\pm1$, so measuring all qubits returns $00\ldots0$ with certainty. For a balanced function the terms cancel exactly, $c_0=0$, and the all-zero outcome never occurs. The decisive step is the second layer of Hadamards, which makes the $2^n$ phase-carrying paths interfere at a single output."),
        ("p", r"The state after the call contains all $2^n$ function values as signs, but a measurement returns only $n$ bits. Holevo's bound forbids extracting more than $n$ classical bits from $n$ qubits. A quantum computer therefore does not evaluate every input and report the results. It gains only when a global property of $f$ can be turned into an interference pattern. The comparison also has a limit. A randomized classical procedure that checks $k$ random inputs errs with probability at most $2^{1-k}$, independent of $n$. The exponential separation holds for exact computation only."),
        ("p", r"The circuit has been run with nuclear spins in liquid-state NMR. Gulde and colleagues (2003) ran it with a single trapped calcium ion, using an electronic transition as one qubit and a vibrational mode as the other. The same pattern, a superposition, a phase oracle and an interfering transform, underlies Simon's algorithm and the Fourier transform in Shor's factoring algorithm."),
        ("p", r"Several facts are established. Universal gate sets exist, as described under quantum circuit. Quantum error correction can protect stored information, and the threshold theorem shows that below a constant error rate per gate, arbitrarily long computations succeed with polylogarithmic overhead. Processors with hundreds of physical qubits operate, and logical qubits whose errors decrease with code size have been demonstrated. Open questions remain: building machines large enough to factor numbers of cryptographic size, and finding practical problems with proven speedups beyond the simulation of quantum systems."),
    ],
    "quantum_cryptography": [
        ("p", r"A banknote that cannot be copied needs a physical feature that no measurement reproduces. Classical features can in principle always be measured precisely and copied. Around 1970 Stephen Wiesner proposed storing, together with a serial number, qubits prepared in randomly chosen states. The proposal, published in 1983, later led to quantum key distribution. Its security rests on the no-cloning theorem."),
        ("p", r"Suppose a unitary $U$ copied two states onto a blank qubit, $U|\psi\rangle|0\rangle=|\psi\rangle|\psi\rangle$ and $U|\phi\rangle|0\rangle=|\phi\rangle|\phi\rangle$. Unitary maps preserve inner products, so"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle\psi|\phi\rangle=\langle\psi|\phi\rangle^2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\langle\psi|\phi\rangle\in\{0,1\}."""),
        ("p", r"Copying is possible only for orthogonal or identical states. For $|0\rangle$ and $|+\rangle=(|0\rangle+|1\rangle)/\sqrt2$ the overlap $1/\sqrt2$ differs from its square $1/2$, so no device copies both. An ancilla or an intermediate measurement does not help. Any physical operation is a unitary on a larger system, and the same inner-product argument applies there."),
        ("p", r"In Wiesner's scheme the bank prepares each of $n$ qubits in one of the four states $|0\rangle$, $|1\rangle$, $|+\rangle$ and $|-\rangle$, chosen at random. It keeps the list of choices secret under the serial number of the note. To verify a note, the bank measures each qubit in its recorded basis, and a genuine note passes with certainty."),
        ("p", r"A counterfeiter with one note tries to produce two notes that both pass. The simplest attack measures each qubit in the $Z$ basis and prepares two copies of the outcome. With the right basis both copies pass; with the wrong basis each copy passes with probability one half. The success probabilities per qubit, and for a whole note, are"),
        ("eq", r"""p_{\rm meas}=\frac12\cdot1+\frac12\cdot\frac14=\frac58,\qquad p_{\rm opt}=\frac34,\qquad
P_{\rm forge}=\Big(\frac34\Big)^n."""),
        ("p", r"The optimum over all physical operations, $3/4$ per qubit, was found by Molina, Vidick and Watrous (2012) using semidefinite programming over channels from one qubit to two. A dual bound and an explicit attack both give $3/4$. They also showed that entangled attacks on several qubits do no better than independent ones. A note of $n=50$ qubits is therefore forged with probability $(3/4)^{50}\approx6\times10^{-7}$."),
        ("p", r"The scheme assumes that only the bank verifies notes and that the bases stay secret. If the bank hands back notes that fail verification, repeated queries reveal the states one qubit at a time (Lutomirski, 2010). Even returning only valid notes permits an attack based on interaction-free measurement. Issuing a fresh note after every verification removes both attacks. A practical bank must also tolerate a small fraction of failed qubits, which raises the forging probability by an amount fixed by the tolerated error."),
        ("p", r"Notes that anyone could verify without the secret list, public-key quantum money, cannot be secure against unbounded adversaries. Proposed constructions rely on computational assumptions whose security against quantum attacks is still being tested. The four Wiesner states and their trade-off between information and disturbance reappear in BB84 quantum key distribution. The counterfeiter's operation is a quantum channel from one qubit to two, constrained by complete positivity."),
    ],
    "quantum_engineering": [
        ("p", r"Two superconducting pads joined by a thin insulating barrier behave as a single quantum degree of freedom, although they contain a macroscopic number of electrons. Such circuits are designed and fabricated like electrical circuits, and quantum engineering chooses their parameters so that the lowest levels form a usable qubit. The transmon, a widely used design, shows these choices in one calculation."),
        ("p", r"Let $n$ be the number of Cooper pairs that have tunnelled across the Josephson junction and $\varphi$ the superconducting phase difference, with $[\varphi,n]=i$. The charging energy $E_C=e^2/(2C)$ sets the cost of charge on the capacitance $C$, and the Josephson energy $E_J$ sets the strength of pair tunnelling. The Hamiltonian is"),
        ("eq", r"""H=4E_C(n-n_g)^2-E_J\cos\varphi."""),
        ("p", r"Here $n_g$ is an offset charge set by the electrostatic environment, which fluctuates in time. For $E_J\gg E_C$ the phase stays near zero. Expanding the cosine to fourth order gives a harmonic oscillator with a quartic correction. With $b$ the oscillator lowering operator and $\varphi=(2E_C/E_J)^{1/4}(b+b^\dagger)$,"),
        ("eq", r"""H\approx\sqrt{8E_JE_C}\Big(b^\dagger b+\frac12\Big)-E_J-\frac{E_C}{12}\big(b+b^\dagger\big)^4."""),
        ("p", r"In lowest order the quartic term shifts level $m$ by $-(E_C/2)(m^2+m+1/2)$. The level spacings therefore decrease with $m$, and the two lowest transitions differ by the anharmonicity $\alpha$:"),
        ("eq", r"""\hbar\omega_{01}\approx\sqrt{8E_JE_C}-E_C,\qquad
\hbar\alpha=\hbar(\omega_{12}-\omega_{01})\approx-E_C."""),
        ("p", r"The anharmonicity turns the oscillator into a qubit. A microwave pulse resonant with $\omega_{01}$ drives the $1\to2$ transition with a detuning $|\alpha|$. Pulses much longer than $1/|\alpha|$ therefore keep the population in the two lowest levels. For $E_C/h=250$ MHz and $E_J/E_C=50$, the formulas give $\omega_{01}/2\pi=4.75$ GHz and $\alpha/2\pi=-250$ MHz. Exact diagonalization in the charge basis gives $4.74$ GHz and $-287$ MHz. Since $1/|\alpha|$ is about $0.6$ ns, pulses of tens of nanoseconds keep leakage small, and shaped pulses suppress it further."),
        ("p", r"The large ratio $E_J/E_C$ is the other design choice. The sensitivity of the levels to the fluctuating offset charge falls as $\exp(-\sqrt{8E_J/E_C})$. In the example, the transition frequency varies with $n_g$ by about 10 kHz. At $E_J/E_C=1$, as in the earlier Cooper-pair box, it varies by almost 800 MHz. The price is a weaker relative anharmonicity, which decreases only as $(8E_J/E_C)^{-1/2}$ (Koch and colleagues, 2007)."),
        ("p", r"The device must also be cold. At $4.74$ GHz the transition energy corresponds to $0.23$ K. At a refrigerator temperature of 20 mK the thermal excited population $e^{-h\nu/k_BT}$ is about $10^{-5}$, although residual excitation measured in devices is often larger. Loss in dielectric interfaces and broken Cooper pairs then limit the coherence times."),
        ("p", r"The transmon is a quantum harmonic oscillator made weakly anharmonic, and its Josephson term describes tunnelling of Cooper pairs through the barrier. Transmons coupled through a common resonator exchange excitations as described under quantum bus."),
    ],
}

